/-!
# Global sections avoiding a finite set of points

A global section `s` of a sheaf of `𝒪ₓ`-modules `F` *vanishes* at a point `x` of `X` when its
germ at `x` lies in `𝔪ₓ Fₓ` (`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.VanishesAt`). This file proves
that on a scheme over an **infinite** field a *single* global section can be chosen which
vanishes at none of a prescribed **finite** set of points, provided that at each of those points
some global section does not vanish, and applies it to the associated points of a coherent
sheaf.

The mechanism is the classical one. Vanishing at a fixed point `x` is a linear condition: the
global sections vanishing at `x` form a `Γ(X, 𝒪ₓ)`-submodule of `Γ(X, F)`, hence a `k`-subspace
for a scheme over `k`, and it is a *proper* subspace exactly when some global section does not
vanish at `x`. A vector space over an infinite field is not the union of finitely many proper
subspaces (`Subspace.exists_forall_notMem`), so a section outside all of them exists.

That some global section does not vanish at `x` is, by Nakayama's lemma
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.exists_not_vanishesAt`), implied by the germs at `x` of the
global sections spanning the stalk `Fₓ`, as soon as `Fₓ` is a non-zero finite `𝒪_{X, x}`-module;
and the germs span the stalk at every point as soon as `F` is generated by its global sections in
the sense of `SheafOfModules.IsGloballyGenerated`, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.isGloballyGeneratedAt_of_isGloballyGenerated`.

The application is to the associated points of a coherent sheaf: on a quasi-compact locally
Noetherian scheme these are finite in number
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.finite_associatedPoints`), so a sheaf generated by its global
sections at each of them has a global section vanishing at none of them. This is the genericity
half of `lem:deg-general-hyperplane` of the blueprint chapter on the degree of the isogeny,
stated without reference to a projective embedding: the blueprint chooses a hyperplane
`∑ⱼ aⱼ Tⱼ` avoiding finitely many points of `𝐏ᴺ` by exactly this argument, and the linear forms
`∑ⱼ aⱼ Tⱼ` are nothing but the global sections of `𝒪(1)|_X`.

For the structure sheaf the hypotheses are automatic and vanishing is the complement of a basic
open subset, so the conclusion takes the concrete form of a global function whose zero locus
avoids the associated points of `F`, and hence — by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.injective_smul_iff_associatedPoints_subset` — acts injectively
on the sections of `F` over every open subset.

## Main results

- `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.exists_forall_not_vanishesAt`: **a global section avoiding
  finitely many points**, over an infinite field.
- `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.exists_forall_associatedPoints_not_vanishesAt`: a global
  section vanishing at no associated point of a coherent sheaf, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.exists_forall_associatedPoints_not_vanishesAt_of_invertible`,
  its specialisation to a globally generated invertible sheaf, where the hypotheses on `L` are
  automatic.
- `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.exists_associatedPoints_subset_basicOpen`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.exists_injective_smul_associatedPoints`: a global *function*
  whose basic open subset contains the associated points of a coherent sheaf, and which therefore
  acts injectively on it.

